REM Protects class code carrying user line numbers: a number directly before a field, before a
REM method header, before a method's own end marker, before the class end marker, and on the
REM class header line itself, plus the same class written with no line numbers at all. Each
REM number sits on its own line immediately ahead of what it numbers. Upper and lower case.
REM Also protects a number sharing the SAME line as a field, a method header, the class end
REM marker, and an interface end marker -- the parser has always accepted this placement; the
REM validator's own line-break check used to draw a false "needs to start in a new line" alarm
REM on the masked keyword.

0010
CLASS PUBLIC Counter
0020
FIELD PUBLIC INTEGER total
0030
METHOD PUBLIC VOID bump()
0040
total = total + 1
0050
METHODEND
0060
CLASSEND

0010
class public tally
0020
field public integer total
0030
method public void bump()
0040
total = total + 1
0050
methodend
0060
classend

class public plaintally
field public integer total
method public void bump()
total = total + 1
methodend
classend

CLASS PUBLIC SameLineNums
0015 FIELD PUBLIC INTEGER total
0016 METHOD PUBLIC VOID bump()
total = total + 1
METHODEND
0060 CLASSEND

INTERFACE PUBLIC SameLineIface
METHOD PUBLIC VOID grow()
0070 INTERFACEEND
